  "result": "Implemented `netgraph/graph.py` and all 3 tests in `tests/test_t1.py` pass with `python -m pytest -q`.\n\nI chose Approach A, adjacency sets. Neighbour iteration proportional to degree makes BFS shortest paths cheap on sparse graphs, and it still gives O(1) `has_edge` via set membership, without the n\u00b2 memory cost of a matrix.\n\nDetails worth knowing:\n\n- **Edges** are stored as one set per node, with a running edge counter so `edge_count` is O(1). Self-loops add the node to its own set once and count once.\n- **Bounds checks** run on every node argument and raise `IndexError`, including for negative ids. A negative node count in the constructor raises `ValueError`.\n- **Shortest path** is a plain BFS that records parents and reconstructs the path once the target is dequeued, returning `[u]` for `u == v` and `None` if unreachable.\n\nNothing was committed. The new file is untracked in the working tree.",
